mall Baroque-style church located on Via Sant'Alessandro, near the port area, in the ancient quarter of Castellammare, in central Palermo, region of Sicily, Italy. The church now hosts Russian Orthodox services.","wpu":"Sant'Alessandro dei Carbonai","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/25/Chiesa_di_Sant%27Alessandro_dei_Carbonai_%28Palermo%29.JPG/960px-Chiesa_di_Sant%27Alessandro_dei_Carbonai_%28Palermo%29.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Foro Italico, Palermo","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":38.1181,"lo":13.3739,"d":609,"ds":"The Foro Italico (Italian Forum) is a pedestrian path and park along the seafront of Palermo, Sicily, Italy. In 1582, viceroy Marcantonio Colonna created a walking path in this part of the coast, that became a favorite destination for the leisure of the upper classes of the city in the 17th and 18th centuries.","wpu":"Foro Italico, Palermo","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/28/Foro_Italico_10.jpg/960px-Foro_Italico_10.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Oratory of San Lorenzo","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":38.116686,"lo":13.366431,"d":632,"ds":"The Oratory of Saint Lawrence (Italian: Oratorio di San Lorenzo) is a Baroque oratory of Palermo. It is located near the Basilica of Saint Francis of Assisi, in the quarter of the Kalsa, within the historic centre of Palermo. The oratory was built in 1569 to replace a former smaller church dedicated to St Lawrence.","wpu":"Oratory of San Lorenzo","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a8/Oratorio_San_Lorenzo_-_Interno.jpg/960px-Oratorio_San_Lorenzo_-_Interno.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Teatro Biondo","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":38.1175,"lo":13.3627,"d":744,"ds":"The Biondo Theatre (Italian: Teatro Biondo, complete name Teatro Biondo Stabile) is a neoclassical and Art Nouveau-style theater building located on Via Roma #258, corner with Via Venezia, in the ancient quarter of Castellamare of central Palermo, region of Sicily, Italy. Diagonally across Via Roma is the ancient church of Sant'Antonio Abate and a stairwell descending into the warrens of the Vucciria Market.","wpu":"Teatro Biondo","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/36/Teatro_Biondo_Palermo.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled"},{"n":"Santa Maria dello Spasimo","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":38.114167,"lo":13.371944,"d":908,"ds":"Santa Maria dello Spasimo, or Lo Spasimo, is an unfinished Catholic church in the Kalsa neighborhood in Palermo, Sicily, on Via dello Spasimo. Construction of the church and accompanying monastery of the Olivetan Order began in 1509 with a papal bull from Julius II, on land bequeathed by Giacomo Basilicò, a lawyer and the widower of a rich noblewoman.","wpu":"Santa Maria dello Spasimo","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f8/Lo_Spasimo_msu2017-0226.jpg/960px-Lo_Spasimo_msu2017-0226.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Santa Ninfa dei Crociferi","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":38.116611,"lo":13.360444,"d":958,"ds":"The Church of Saint Nympha (Italian: Chiesa di Santa Ninfa or Santa Ninfa dei Crociferi) is a Baroque-Mannerist church of Palermo. The facade rises on Via Maqueda, a block north of the central intersection known as the Quattro Canti, in the quarter of Seralcadi, within the historic centre of Palermo. The church belongs to the Camillians (also known as \"Crociferi\").","wpu":"Santa Ninfa dei Crociferi","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/40/Santa_Ninfa_dei_Crociferi%2C_Palermo.jpg/960px-Santa_Ninfa_dei_Crociferi%2C_Palermo.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Province of Palermo","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":38.115556,"lo":13.361389,"d":978,"ds":"The province of Palermo (Italian: provincia di Palermo; Sicilian: pruvincia di Palermu) was a province in the autonomous region of Sicily, a major island in Southern Italy. Its capital was the city of Palermo. On 4 August 2015, it was replaced by the Metropolitan City of Palermo.","wpu":"Province of Palermo","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d4/Palermo_in_Italy.svg/960px-Palermo_in_Italy.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Church of San Cataldo","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":38.1148,"lo":13.3625,"d":981,"ds":"The Church of San Cataldo (or Saint Cathal's Church) is a Catholic church located at Piazza Bellini, in central Palermo, Sicily, Italy. Erected in 1154 as a notable example of the Arab-Norman architecture which flourished in Sicily under Norman rule on the island, the church is annexed to that of Santa Maria dell'Ammiraglio. Since the 1930s, it belongs to the Order of the Holy Sepulchre.","wpu":"Church of San Cataldo","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0e/Chiesa_di_San_Cataldo_palermo.JPG/960px-Chiesa_di_San_Cataldo_palermo.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Palazzo Riso","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":38.115278,"lo":13.36,"d":1085,"ds":"The Palazzo Riso, officially RISO, Museo d'Arte Contemporanea della Sicilia (\"RISO, Contemporary Art Museum of Sicily\") and also known as Palazzo Belmonte Riso, is a regional contemporary art museum located in the ancient Corso Vittorio Emanuele, Palermo, Italy.","wpu":"Palazzo Riso","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Palazzo_Blemonte_Riso.jpg/960px-Palazzo_Blemonte_Riso.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Sant'Agostino, Palermo","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":38.118108,"lo":13.357578,"d":1088,"ds":"The Church of Saint Augustine (Italian: Chiesa di Sant'Agostino) is a Gothic church of Palermo. It is located near the market of the Capo, in the quarter of the Seralcadio, within the historic centre of Palermo. The church is also called Santa Rita, because of the devotion to this Augustinian saint.","wpu":"Sant'Agostino, Palermo","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e5/Palermo-San-Agostino-bjs.jpg/960px-Palermo-San-Agostino-bjs.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Orto botanico di Palermo","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":38.1125,"lo":13.3725,"d":1099,"ds":"The Orto Botanico di Palermo (Palermo Botanical Garden) is both a botanical garden and a research and educational institution of the Department of Botany of the University of Palermo. The garden lies within the city of Palermo, Italy at 10 m (33 ft) above sea-level. It covers about 0.12 km2 (30 acres) on top of red soil that has evolved on a limestone tuff substratum.","wpu":"Orto botanico di Palermo","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/4e/Orto_botanico_Palermo_04.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled"},{"n":"Fontana del Genio a Villa Giulia","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":38.113056,"lo":13.374444,"d":1102,"ds":"The Fontana del Genio a Villa Giulia or Genio di Villa Giulia is a sculpted water fountain in the park of the Villa Giulia in Palermo. It was sculpted by Ignazio Marabitti in 1778 and shows the Genius of Palermo.","wpu":"Fontana del Genio a Villa Giulia","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/43/GenioPalermoMarabitti.JPG/960px-GenioPalermoMarabitti.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"}],"about":{"ds":"Palermo (Sicilian: Palermu) is the capital and largest city of the autonomous island region of Sicily in southern Italy, located on the eponymous gulf facing the Tyrrhenian Sea. A 2,700-year-old city, it is noted for its history, culture, architecture and gastronomy, playing an important role throughout much of its existence. Founded in 734 BC by the Phoenicians as Sis (\"flower\"), Palermo then became a possession of Carthage.","wpu":"Palermo","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/c7/Sicilia_Palermo5_tango7174.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled"},"wk":"2026-10-18"}